--- docs/architecture/buster-fixture-cleanup.md
+++ docs/architecture/buster-fixture-cleanup.md
@@ -10,6 +10,10 @@
 
 A replacement provider process receives the host disposition explicitly. It cannot infer success from its own memory because execution may have occurred in a different process. This same-host session recovery is distinct from durable crash recovery of failed cleanup: the loader still disposes its package snapshot in its existing finally boundary. No durable cleanup retry ledger is added here.
 
+Normal successful fixture cleanup can use the original live provider session. A replacement is permitted only after the currently owned session has both actually closed and completed capability drain. A signal failure may still be reported after these facts are confirmed; that diagnostic does not erase disposal confirmation. A close or drain timeout leaves disposal unconfirmed and cannot authorize another child. Each replacement becomes the current owned session before initialization. CPU usage is accumulated once per confirmed session, with peak memory/process values combined by maximum, including overlapping stop calls.
+
+Cleanup and subsequent stop failures remain distinct causes. When both occur, the loader preserves both in an AggregateError and its public message; retained cleanup records the diagnostic in cleanupErrors. Unconfirmed disposal still rejects rather than declaring a safe cleanup. A failed stop does not authorize package-snapshot deletion. The final Worker summary continues prioritizing a later cleanup failure over an earlier execution failure; the earlier preparation/rollback marker is observable at the execution IPC boundary, not guaranteed to be repeated in the final summary. This does not claim every combination of internal signal, close-timeout and drain-timeout errors is newly aggregated.
+
 Old immutable Kubernetes provider snapshots retain their old source behavior and cannot be repaired by merely supplying an extra argument. Historical cleanup safety requires a separate compatibility/ownership contract. This change does not rewrite snapshots, change grants, authorize cluster operations or claim that historical namespaces have been retired.
 
 `npm run verify:buster:kubernetes-cleanup` includes the original child/ownership/plugin tests and local regressions through the real invoker, registered provider and Runner. Their injected CLI/OS failure boundaries must be reported explicitly; they are not live cluster or kernel-fault acceptance. Current execution/review status belongs to the accompanying remediation evidence, not this behavioral reference.
